The flag evaluation path runs on every request that touches a flagged feature, so it must be fast, reliable, and safe when the flag service is unreachable. For production Java services, a dedicated platform like LaunchDarkly, Unleash (open-source), or Flagsmith handles the hard parts: local caching of flag state, streaming updates via SSE, audit logs, and typed evaluation methods.

The SDK evaluates flags locally against a cached ruleset — latency is sub-millisecond, not a network round-trip. The flag service pushes updates to connected SDKs in real time. When the service is unreachable, the SDK falls back to the last known state or a hardcoded default. That default must be the conservative choice: **the feature stays off**.

```java
@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    private final LDClient ldClient;

    public CheckoutResponse checkout(User user, Cart cart) {
        LDContext context = LDContext.builder(user.getId())
            .set("email", user.getEmail())
            .set("plan", user.getPlan())
            .build();

        // Default false = feature hidden if flag service is down
        if (ldClient.boolVariation("new-checkout-flow", context, false)) {
            return newCheckoutFlow(user, cart);
        }
        return legacyCheckoutFlow(user, cart);
    }
}
```

The `LDContext` carries user attributes that targeting rules evaluate against. The boolean default `false` means an unreachable flag service keeps users on the safe legacy path rather than accidentally exposing unfinished code.

An open circuit breaker without a useful fallback is just a different kind of error. Instead of a timeout, the caller gets a `CallNotPermittedException`. If that propagates to the user as a 500, the experience is not meaningfully better — it is just faster at failing.

Useful fallback strategies depend on the dependency's role. A product recommendation service can return an empty list or cached results. A payment processor can queue the request for asynchronous retry and return a "processing" status. A rate-limiting service can fail open — allow the request rather than blocking it — because false accepts are less damaging than false rejects during an outage.

```java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "recommendations", fallbackMethod = "cachedRecommendations")
public List<Product> getRecommendations(String userId) {
    return recommendationClient.fetch(userId);
}

public List<Product> cachedRecommendations(String userId, Exception ex) {
    log.warn("Recommendation circuit open, serving cache", ex);
    return recommendationCache.getOrDefault(userId, Collections.emptyList());
}
```

The fallback method signature must match the original's return type and accept the exception as an additional parameter. Resilience4j routes to this method whenever the circuit is open or the call fails.

The **composition order with retries** deserves emphasis because getting it backwards is a common production mistake. Retry should wrap the circuit breaker, not the reverse. When retry is the outer decorator, one failed call retries three times, and the circuit breaker sees one failure — the final outcome. When retry is inside the circuit breaker, three retry attempts of one failed call count as three separate failures, tripping the breaker three times faster than intended.

The readiness probe answers a different question: can this pod handle requests right now? A pod starting up and warming caches is alive but not ready. A pod whose database connection pool is exhausted is alive but should not receive new requests. A pod shutting down gracefully is alive but must stop receiving new traffic.

Spring Boot Actuator exposes `/actuator/health/readiness`, which aggregates readiness-relevant health indicators. During graceful shutdown, Spring Boot automatically marks the readiness state as `REFUSING_TRAFFIC`, causing the probe to return 503. Kubernetes removes the pod from Service endpoints, and the load balancer stops routing — exactly the behavior needed for zero-downtime deployment.

For custom readiness checks, implement a `HealthIndicator` bean that validates business-critical dependencies. Not every dependency needs a readiness check — only the ones whose unavailability means the pod genuinely cannot serve requests.

```java
@Component
public class PaymentGatewayHealth implements HealthIndicator {
    private final PaymentClient client;

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try {
            client.ping();  // lightweight connectivity check
            return Health.up().build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("service", "payment-gateway")
                .withDetail("error", e.getMessage())
                .build();
        }
    }
}
```

This indicator automatically aggregates into the readiness endpoint. When the payment gateway is unreachable, readiness fails and Kubernetes stops sending traffic. The pod stays alive — liveness is unaffected — so when the gateway recovers, the pod passes readiness again and traffic resumes without a restart.
